.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 233 PM PST Mon Nov 9 2026
(This evening through Tuesday night)

Upper level ridging prevails over the West Coast (and most of the
United States) heading into Tuesday. Expect foggy conditions to
develop across the North Bay Valleys, along the San Francisco and
San Pablo Bayshores and bays, the delta, and potentially the
interior East Bay Valleys. The conditions are favorable for fog to
develop again tonight with decent moisture and good overnight
radiational cooling thanks to a lack of mid/high level clouds.
Bayshore locations and any mariners on the San Francisco/San Pablo
Bays or the Delta should be prepared for fog, likely dense, to
develop early Tuesday morning. If driving, remember to slow down and
allow extra time to reach your destination. If boating in fog, turn
on all running lights, slow down, and listen for any other boats
that may be nearby. As of 1PM Monday, visibilities are still
struggling to improve along portions of the bay shoreline from this
morning`s fog. Tomorrow will likely be similar with reduced
visibilities and patchy fog persisting into the late morning/early
afternoon hours.

Despite early morning fog, high temperatures will warm back into the
70s across the interior and 60s to 70s along the coast by Tuesday
afternoon. This is brought to us by persistent upper level ridging
over the Western US and 850 mb temperatures around 12-16 C. While
pleasantly warm, these temperatures are around 10 degrees above
normal for this time of year. Morning low temperatures remain in the
50s with slightly cooler lows in the 40s across the North Bay. While
the high temperatures don`t make it feel like winter is coming,
there is a pattern change expected late week into the weekend.

