.LONG TERM...
Issued at 233 PM PST Mon Nov 9 2026
(Wednesday through next Sunday)

Upper level ridging and above normal temperatures continue Wednesday
and Thursday. High temperatures remain in the 70s on Wednesday
across the interior but trend cooler on Thursday (dropping into the
60s to low 70s). Coastal areas remain constant in the 60s to low
70s. Unsettled weather returns Friday and brings with it a host of
topics to discuss. Upper level ridging will be replaced by a deep
upper level trough pushing into the West Coast. Temperatures will
cool, becoming more seasonal to seasonally cool. Interior highs will
be in the mid to upper 60s Friday/Saturday before dropping into the
low 60s Sunday/Monday. Coastal areas will be in the upper 50s to mid
60s. Overnight lows will cool as well with high temperatures
gradually dropping into the 40s to upper 30s across the region.

Initially it looked as though we could see rain as early as
Wednesday/Thursday but those chances have evaporated - thanks to a
cutoff low splitting from the main trough and pushing into Southern
California and avoiding us. Rain chances now increase Friday into
the weekend with beneficial rain expected across the Bay Area and
Central Coast. The active period looks to continue well past Monday
with ensemble guidance highlighting many additional troughs/rounds
of rain arriving during the second half of the month. No major
flooding concerns are expected with this weekend`s storm but that
may change over the next few weeks if we do see several rounds of
storms over a short time frame. There is even a low (5-12%) chance
of thunderstorms this weekend as the bulk of the rain moves in.
Gusty winds (30-40 mph) are likely as the front passes. The current
forecast stays below Wind Advisory criteria but that will be
something to keep an eye on as we head closer to the weekend. Now
would be a good time to secure any outdoor decorations or bring in
any outdoor furniture. If you have any other winter/rain
preparations to do (i.e. clearing your gutters, power outage prep,
trimming tree limbs) make sure to do it before Friday.

Hazardous beach conditions return late week into the weekend as long
period NW swell and large breaking waves. The current forecast has
wave heights between 18 to 22 feet across the outer waters which
will bring hazardous conditions for mariners. Beachgoers will also
experience hazardous conditions due to large breaking waves, sneaker
waves, and strong rip currents. This is not a good weekend to go to
the beach.&&

