.BEACHES...
Issued at 330 AM PST Mon Nov 9 2026

Beach Hazards Statement:

Beach Hazard Statement remains in effect. Latest buoy obs show
the moderate swell and moderate period continue to produce an
energetic surf zone. As such, large shore break, sneaker waves,
and stronger rip currents will be possible. Breaking waves of 12
to 16 feet are expected at exposed beaches and up to 18 ft at
favored break point. Spring tides will also increase the currents
and bring higher tides than normal. Waves can sweep across the
shoreline without warning, pulling people into the sea from
beaches, jetties, and rocks. Stay off of jetties, piers, rocks,
and other waterside infrastructure. Remain out of the water if
you`re not an experienced swimmer to avoid hazardous surf and
never turn your back on the ocean! There may be a lull in wave
activity midweek, but longer range forecasts show additional
swells impacting the coast next weekend.

Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion from
warmer than normal water will result in tidal flooding concerns
across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco, San Pablo, and
Monterey Bays. At the San Francisco tide gauge, high tide is
expected to be 1.6 ft above normal (7.4 ft MLLW) at 10:35 AM Monday
11/9, 1.5 ft above normal (7.3 ft MLLW) at 11:04 AM Tuesday 11/10,
1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 11:35 AM Wednesday 11/11, 1.4
ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 12:10 PM Thursday 11/12, 1.2 ft
above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 12:49 PM Friday 11/13. These
predictions include up to 1.5 ft of surge that will enhance the
astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to 90
minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast and through the San
Francisco Bay, respectively. In addition, long-period swell will
create hazardous conditions along the Pacific coast beaches with an
increased risk of sneaker waves and rip currents.

These Spring Tides are due to a new moon on Monday, which is much
lower in the sky this time of year compared to a full moon. In 2
weeks, the astronomical tides will be about 0.8` higher than this
round, and the total water level could be more than a foot higher if
we get SW winds and/or lower pressure. This combination would bring
the total water level near the 1% annual exceedance probability
(2.85` above normal), or a 1 in 100 year event. Something to keep an
eye on for the week of Thanksgiving.

